  "sections": [
    {
      "phase": "reference",
      "title": "Yak 18T Flight Report — General Description",
      "criticality": "normal",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "General",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A four-seat tourer-cum-aerobatic trainer, the 18T was designed by Yuri Yankyevich and first flew in 1967.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "More than a thousand have been produced and delivered, mainly to Eastern Bloc countries. The manufacturing licence was subsequently bought by TechnoAvia in Moscow.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Nine-cylinder supercharged Vedeneyev 360hp M-14P radial engine.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "At 27 1/2 feet long and 36 1/2 feet span, the Yak is nearly four feet longer and a foot wider than a Piper Warrior.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Construction",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Riveted alloy panels form the main structure. The rudder, double-braced tailplane and elevator are fabric covered.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The split elevator has an adjustable trim-tab on each side, while the rudder carries only a fixed tab.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The wing centre-section carries an oil radiator in the starboard wing-root and a large metal single-position plain flap-cum airbrake across its span, plus the inward-retracting main gear legs.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The battery is mounted on a hinged tray in the port wing-root, released by three Camlock fasteners, and may be dropped down for service.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "External power connector behind a small access door near the port wing-root.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Wings and Fuel",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The wing dihedral starts at the break point just outboard of the main undercarriage. The outer wing panels are fabric-covered and can be removed.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The port wing carries a long barber's-pole-striped pitot tube, the stall warning vane, and a large-diameter landing light. The Frieze ailerons are also fabric-covered, while tie-down rings are provided near each wingtip.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Each wing contains a 95 litre (21 imperial gallon) fuel tank, of which four litres are unusable.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Undercarriage",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "On each of the three gear legs is a small, forward-facing white light for the benefit of the flight dispatcher to confirm undercarriage down at night.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Three small mechanical plungers protrude — one from the top surface of each wing and one just in front of the windscreen — to act as a belt-and-braces method of checking the undercarriage position.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Main wheels are 5.00 x 500. Long anti-static wicks trail from each main wheel assembly.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Cockpit and Equipment",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Each door is individually jettisonable via a prominent red handle below the panel. The aircraft can be flown with both doors removed.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The front seats are fore-and-aft adjustable. Four-point harness is fitted; no central fifth g-strap nor second aerobatic security strap is provided.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The hinged rudder pedals have leather stirrups but no brake pedals. The pedal assemblies are adjustable.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Rear passengers have a wide bench seat behind which sits a large yellow-painted auxiliary fuel tank holding 95 litres, which drains automatically into the wing-tanks.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The cockpit trim and rear seat materials are reminiscent of an early 1960s aeroplane.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The rear side windows boast sliding curtains, also fitted above the two front seats. Two smoked plastic sun visors hinge down from the top of the screens.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A pull-cord arrangement unrolls a miniature window-blind in front of the pilot's screen to facilitate IMC training.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A two-speed, self-parking windscreen wiper is fitted, but no washers.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The panel is dominated centre-stage by a huge electrically-driven and non-toppling artificial horizon.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A gyro-magnetic compass is adjustable for north or south hemispheres and individual latitudes.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Fuel System",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A single fuel gauge with a selector switch shows total litres in both tanks on the outer scale, and the contents of the selected tank on the inner scale.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The fuel is controlled by a prominent red T-handle on the panel left of the quadrant. Push for On, pull for Off.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Electrical System",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A combined single ammeter/voltmeter is fitted; press its switch for amps, release for volts.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Controls",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Each yoke sports two PTT buttons; the left-hand one opens the air-ground transmission circuit, the right-hand one activates the cockpit intercom.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The pneumatically-operated brakes work through a pull-handle on the back of each yoke. The P2 position has an override button to allow the instructor to release the brakes.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The park brake is a small button under the yoke; pull the lever and press the button.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "No nose-wheel steering: to turn on the ground, press the appropriate rudder pedal and squeeze the brake lever.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The pitch trimmer is a small handle on the left-hand cockpit wall, low-geared taking five turns from full aft to full nose-down trim. A light indicates neutral trim.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Annunciation and Warning Lights",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Annunciation lights show Fuel low and Gen. fail. Two red lights flash Slow and Stall as the aircraft approaches stalling speed.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Undercarriage and Flap Controls",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The undercarriage lever is set low down on the left-hand end of the panel, pressed firmly before operation, with a slide-across safety bar.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The two-position (Up and Down) flap lever is on the central quadrant with the cowl gills control, carb heat, oil cooler selector and throttle friction lever.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The prop lever actuates a microswitch which, at anything less than full throttle in fully fine setting, illuminates the landing gear warning light.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "phase": "engine_start",
      "title": "Starting",
      "criticality": "normal",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The big radial requires to be thoroughly warm before flight.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "For really cold climates the engine oil can be diluted by a button next to the starter, feeding neat petrol into the sump, up to twelve per cent dilution.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "action",
          "text": "Primer",
          "response": "SEVERAL PUMPS",
          "tickable": true,
